window.NOTES={"pure":{"p1_1":"### The idea\nAn index (power) tells you how many times to multiply a number by itself: $a^3=a\\times a\\times a$. The index laws are just shortcuts for counting how many $a$'s you have. Multiplying $a^2\\times a^3$ gives five $a$'s, so you **add** the powers. Dividing cancels some of them, so you **subtract**.\nNegative and fractional powers follow from keeping these laws consistent. Since $a^3\\div a^5=a^{-2}$ and also $=\\dfrac1{a^2}$, a negative power means \"one over\". Since $a^{\\frac12}\\times a^{\\frac12}=a^1$, the power $\\tfrac12$ must mean square root.\nA **surd** is an irrational root such as $\\sqrt2$. We keep surds exact instead of rounding them.\n### Key results\n- $a^m\\times a^n=a^{m+n}$, $a^m\\div a^n=a^{m-n}$, $(a^m)^n=a^{mn}$, $a^0=1$\n- $a^{-n}=\\dfrac1{a^n}$, $a^{\\frac1n}=\\sqrt[n]a$, $a^{\\frac mn}=(\\sqrt[n]a)^m$\n- $\\sqrt{ab}=\\sqrt a\\sqrt b$ and $\\sqrt{\\tfrac ab}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt a}{\\sqrt b}$ — but $\\sqrt{a+b}\\neq\\sqrt a+\\sqrt b$\n- To rationalise $\\dfrac{1}{a+\\sqrt b}$, multiply the top and bottom by $a-\\sqrt b$. The bottom becomes $a^2-b$ (difference of two squares).\n### Worked example\n**Simplify $\\dfrac{8^{\\frac23}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\times x^2$.** Since $8^{\\frac23}=(\\sqrt[3]8)^2=4$ and $\\sqrt x=x^{\\frac12}$, the expression is $4x^{2-\\frac12}=4x^{\\frac32}$.\n**Rationalise $\\dfrac{3}{2-\\sqrt3}$.** Multiply by $\\dfrac{2+\\sqrt3}{2+\\sqrt3}$: $\\dfrac{3(2+\\sqrt3)}{4-3}=6+3\\sqrt3$.\n### Watch out\n- $(2x)^3=8x^3$, not $2x^3$ — the power applies to everything in the bracket.\n- Before differentiating or integrating, rewrite $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt x}$ as $3x^{-\\frac12}$.\n- Simplify surds fully: $\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt2$.","p1_2":"### The idea\nA quadratic $ax^2+bx+c$ has a U-shaped graph (a parabola), or an upside-down U if $a<0$. Solving $ax^2+bx+c=0$ means finding where this graph crosses the $x$-axis.\nCompleting the square rewrites the quadratic as $a(x+p)^2+q$. Because a square can never be negative, you can read off the smallest (or largest) value straight away: it is $q$, when $x=-p$. That point is the **vertex**.\nThe **discriminant** $b^2-4ac$ is the part under the square root in the quadratic formula. If it is negative, the square root doesn't exist, so the equation has no real roots.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"110\"/><path d=\"M30 20 Q90 190 150 20\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"160\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">b²−4ac &gt; 0: two roots</text><line x1=\"185\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"110\"/><path d=\"M205 20 Q265 200 325 20\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"195\" y=\"160\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">b²−4ac = 0: one root</text><line x1=\"360\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"110\"/><path d=\"M380 10 Q440 150 500 10\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"365\" y=\"160\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">b²−4ac &lt; 0: no roots</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- Quadratic formula: $x=\\dfrac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}$\n- $x^2+bx+c=\\left(x+\\tfrac b2\\right)^2-\\left(\\tfrac b2\\right)^2+c$\n- The vertex of $y=a(x+p)^2+q$ is $(-p,q)$, and the line of symmetry is $x=-p$.\n- The graph crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,c)$.\n### Worked example\n**Write $2x^2-12x+5$ in the form $a(x+p)^2+q$ and state the minimum point.**\nTake out the 2: $2(x^2-6x)+5=2[(x-3)^2-9]+5=2(x-3)^2-13$. The minimum point is $(3,-13)$.\n**For which $k$ does $x^2+kx+9=0$ have equal roots?** Set $b^2-4ac=0$: $k^2-36=0$, so $k=\\pm6$.\n### Watch out\n- When completing the square with $a\\neq1$, take $a$ out as a factor first.\n- \"Real roots\" covers both cases, so it means $b^2-4ac\\ge0$.\n- Hidden quadratics such as $x^4-5x^2+4=0$: substitute $u=x^2$.","p1_3":"### The idea\nSimultaneous equations ask where two graphs meet. With two straight lines there is usually one answer. With a line and a curve, substitute the line into the curve. You get a quadratic, and the number of solutions it has is the number of intersection points.\nInequalities describe a range of values instead of a single value. For a quadratic inequality, find where the expression equals zero (the **critical values**), sketch the graph, and decide which part is above or below the axis.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"100\"/><path d=\"M60 10 Q180 220 300 10\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"105\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"255\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"95\" y=\"125\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">a</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"125\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">b</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"150\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">below axis: a &lt; x &lt; b</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"30\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">above: x&lt;a or x&gt;b</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number reverses the sign.\n- $(x-a)(x-b)<0$ (with $a<b$) gives $a<x<b$; $(x-a)(x-b)>0$ gives $x<a$ or $x>b$.\n- Set notation: $\\{x:a<x<b\\}$; \"or\" becomes $\\{x:x<a\\}\\cup\\{x:x>b\\}$.\n- On graphs, use dashed lines for $<$ and $>$, and solid lines for $\\le$ and $\\ge$.\n### Worked example\n**Solve $x^2-x-6>0$.** Factorise: $(x-3)(x+2)>0$. The critical values are $-2$ and $3$. The U-shaped graph is above the axis outside the roots, so $x<-2$ or $x>3$.\n**Solve $y=x+1$ and $x^2+y^2=13$.** Substitute: $x^2+(x+1)^2=13$, so $2x^2+2x-12=0$, giving $x=2$ or $-3$. The solutions are $(2,3)$ and $(-3,-2)$.\n### Watch out\n- Don't multiply both sides by $x$ unless you know $x$ is positive.\n- Always give the matching $y$-values with your $x$-values.","p1_4":"### The idea\nSketching a curve means showing its shape and its key features: where it crosses the axes, its turning points, its asymptotes and what happens as $x$ gets large. For a polynomial, the factors give the $x$-intercepts. A squared factor means the curve **touches** the axis and turns back.\n**Transformations** move or stretch a graph you already know. Changes outside the function, like $f(x)+a$ or $af(x)$, affect $y$ in the way you'd expect. Changes inside the function, like $f(x+a)$ or $f(ax)$, affect $x$ and work the opposite way: $f(x+2)$ moves the graph **left** by 2.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 380 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"120\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"165\"/><path d=\"M110 165 Q150 60 190 120 T270 75\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M70 165 Q110 60 150 120 T230 75\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/><text x=\"235\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#1a7f45\" stroke=\"none\">y = f(x)</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#d97706\" stroke=\"none\">y = f(x+40): shifted left</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $y=f(x)+a$: translate by $\\binom0a$. $y=f(x+a)$: translate by $\\binom{-a}0$.\n- $y=af(x)$: vertical stretch, scale factor $a$. $y=f(ax)$: horizontal stretch, scale factor $\\tfrac1a$.\n- $y=-f(x)$: reflect in the $x$-axis. $y=f(-x)$: reflect in the $y$-axis.\n- $y=\\dfrac kx$ has asymptotes $x=0$ and $y=0$. $y=\\dfrac k{x^2}$ stays on one side of the $x$-axis.\n### Worked example\n**Sketch $y=x^2(x-3)$.** It crosses the $y$-axis at $0$. It touches the $x$-axis at $x=0$ (double root) and crosses at $x=3$. The leading term is $+x^3$, so the curve goes from bottom left to top right.\n**The point $(4,6)$ lies on $y=f(x)$. Where is it on $y=2f(x-1)$?** Move it right by 1 and double the $y$-value: $(5,12)$.\n### Watch out\n- Apply only the change the question gives, and state the new coordinates of key points and asymptotes.\n- A repeated root means the curve touches the axis; it does not cross it.","p1_5":"### The idea\nA straight line has a constant gradient: the change in $y$ for each 1 step in $x$. Every line can be written as $y=mx+c$, where $m$ is the gradient and $c$ is where it crosses the $y$-axis. If you know one point and the gradient, the quickest form is $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$.\nPerpendicular lines meet at $90^\\circ$. Rotating a line through a right angle turns the gradient $m$ into $-\\tfrac1m$, which is why the two gradients multiply to $-1$.\n### Key results\n- Gradient $m=\\dfrac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}$.\n- Parallel lines have equal $m$; perpendicular lines satisfy $m_1m_2=-1$.\n- Midpoint $\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}2,\\frac{y_1+y_2}2\\right)$; length $\\sqrt{(\\Delta x)^2+(\\Delta y)^2}$.\n- The form $ax+by+c=0$ uses integers.\n### Worked example\n**Find the perpendicular bisector of $A(1,2)$ and $B(5,10)$.** The midpoint is $(3,6)$. The gradient of $AB$ is $\\frac{8}{4}=2$, so the perpendicular gradient is $-\\frac12$. The line is $y-6=-\\frac12(x-3)$, which gives $x+2y-15=0$.\n### Watch out\n- In modelling questions, interpret the gradient as a rate, e.g. \"cost per extra item\".\n- Area of a triangle formed with the axes: find both intercepts, then use $\\tfrac12\\times$ base $\\times$ height.","p1_6":"### The idea\nA circle is every point at the same distance $r$ from a centre $(a,b)$. Using Pythagoras on the distance from $(x,y)$ to the centre gives the equation $(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2$.\nIf the equation is expanded, like $x^2+y^2-6x+4y-3=0$, complete the square in $x$ and in $y$ to find the centre and radius.\nTwo facts solve most problems. A **tangent is perpendicular to the radius** at the point it touches. The **perpendicular bisector of any chord passes through the centre**.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"100\" r=\"70\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"179.5\" y2=\"50.5\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"1\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"131\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"135\" y=\"118\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">centre (a,b)</text><text x=\"145\" y=\"85\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">r</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#d97706\" stroke=\"none\">tangent ⟂ radius</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- Centre $(a,b)$ and radius $r$: $(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2$.\n- Line meets circle: substitute and use the discriminant — 2 points, 1 point (tangent), or none.\n- Tangent gradient $=-\\dfrac1{\\text{radius gradient}}$.\n- The angle in a semicircle is $90^\\circ$; the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is a diameter of its circumcircle.\n### Worked example\n**Find the centre and radius of $x^2+y^2-6x+4y-3=0$.** Complete the square: $(x-3)^2-9+(y+2)^2-4-3=0$, so $(x-3)^2+(y+2)^2=16$. The centre is $(3,-2)$ and the radius is $4$.\n**Find the tangent at $P(3,2)$.** The radius gradient is $\\frac{2-(-2)}{3-3}$, which is undefined, so the radius is vertical. The tangent is therefore horizontal: $y=2$.\n### Watch out\n- $r^2$ must be positive. For $x^2+y^2-2x+k=0$ you need $1-k>0$.\n- The centre of $(x+3)^2$ is at $x=-3$ — watch the sign.","p1_7":"### The idea\nPolynomials behave like whole numbers: you can divide them, and some divide exactly. The **factor theorem** gives a quick check. If substituting $x=a$ makes $f(x)$ equal 0, then $(x-a)$ is a factor. This is because $f(x)=(x-a)q(x)$, and at $x=a$ the first bracket is zero.\nOnce you've found one factor of a cubic, divide by it to get a quadratic, then factorise that.\n**Proof** means showing something is true for every case, not just checking a few. You can argue from known facts (deduction), check every possible case (exhaustion), or disprove a statement with a single counter-example.\n### Key results\n- $f(a)=0\\iff(x-a)$ is a factor; $f\\!\\left(\\tfrac ba\\right)=0\\iff(ax-b)$ is a factor.\n- Even numbers: $2n$. Odd numbers: $2n+1$. Consecutive integers: $n,n+1$.\n- Cancel algebraic fractions only after factorising: $\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x^2+3x}=\\dfrac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x(x+3)}=\\dfrac{x-3}{x}$.\n### Worked example\n**Fully factorise $f(x)=x^3-2x^2-5x+6$.** $f(1)=1-2-5+6=0$, so $(x-1)$ is a factor. Dividing gives $f(x)=(x-1)(x^2-x-6)=(x-1)(x-3)(x+2)$.\n**Prove that the sum of two consecutive odd numbers is a multiple of 4.** $(2n+1)+(2n+3)=4n+4=4(n+1)$, which is a multiple of 4.\n### Watch out\n- In a proof, write a concluding sentence.\n- \"Show that $(x+2)$ is a factor\" means you must show $f(-2)=0$, not $f(2)=0$.","p1_8":"### The idea\nExpanding $(a+b)^n$ by hand gets slow quickly. Each term comes from choosing $b$ from some of the $n$ brackets and $a$ from the rest. The number of ways to choose $b$ from $r$ brackets is $\\binom nr$, which gives the coefficients. These coefficients are also the rows of Pascal's triangle.\n### Key results\n- $(a+b)^n=a^n+\\binom n1a^{n-1}b+\\binom n2a^{n-2}b^2+\\dots+b^n$\n- General term: $\\binom nr a^{n-r}b^r$.\n- $\\binom nr=\\dfrac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}$; e.g. $\\binom52=10$.\n### Worked example\n**Find the coefficient of $x^3$ in $(2-3x)^5$.** The term is $\\binom53(2)^2(-3x)^3=10\\times4\\times(-27x^3)$, so the coefficient is $-1080$.\n**Use the first three terms of $(1+x)^8$ to estimate $1.01^8$.** $(1+x)^8\\approx1+8x+28x^2$. With $x=0.01$ this gives $1.0828$.\n### Watch out\n- Keep the bracket: $(-3x)^3=-27x^3$.\n- The estimate only works well when $x$ is small.","p1_9":"### The idea\nSOHCAHTOA only works for right-angled triangles. The **sine rule** and **cosine rule** work for any triangle.\nUse the cosine rule when you know two sides and the angle between them (to find the third side), or all three sides (to find an angle). Use the sine rule when you know an opposite side–angle pair.\nThe graphs of $\\sin$, $\\cos$ and $\\tan$ come from the unit circle: the point at angle $\\theta$ is $(\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)$.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><polygon points=\"20,150 230,150 90,30\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"165\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">A</text><text x=\"232\" y=\"165\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">B</text><text x=\"85\" y=\"22\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">C</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"168\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">c</text><text x=\"168\" y=\"90\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">a</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"90\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">b</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $a^2=b^2+c^2-2bc\\cos A$, or rearranged: $\\cos A=\\dfrac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{2bc}$.\n- $\\dfrac{a}{\\sin A}=\\dfrac{b}{\\sin B}=\\dfrac{c}{\\sin C}$.\n- Area $=\\tfrac12ab\\sin C$.\n- Ambiguous case: if $\\sin B=k$, then $B$ could be $\\sin^{-1}k$ or $180^\\circ-\\sin^{-1}k$.\n### Worked example\n**In triangle $ABC$, $a=7$, $b=5$, $c=6$. Find $A$.** $\\cos A=\\frac{25+36-49}{60}=0.2$, so $A=78.5^\\circ$.\n### Watch out\n- Draw the triangle and label sides opposite their matching angles.\n- Bearings are measured clockwise from north; draw a north line at each point.","p1_10":"### The idea\nTrig functions repeat, so an equation like $\\sin x=0.5$ has many solutions. Your calculator gives one (the principal value). Use the graph's symmetry or the CAST diagram to find the others in the interval.\nFor a transformed angle such as $\\sin(2x)$, change the interval first. If $0\\le x<360^\\circ$, then $0\\le 2x<720^\\circ$. Find every value of $2x$ in that range, then halve them.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 380 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"75\"/><path d=\"M20 75 C60 -10 120 -10 160 75 S260 160 300 75\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"45\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"45\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#d97706\"/><circle cx=\"138\" cy=\"45\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"100\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">30°</text><text x=\"125\" y=\"100\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">150°</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#d97706\" stroke=\"none\">sin x = 0.5</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $\\sin x=k$: $x=\\alpha$ or $180^\\circ-\\alpha$. $\\cos x=k$: $x=\\pm\\alpha$. $\\tan x=k$: $x=\\alpha+180^\\circ n$.\n- $\\tan\\theta\\equiv\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$ and $\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta\\equiv1$.\n- Exact values: $\\sin30^\\circ=\\tfrac12$, $\\cos30^\\circ=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2$, $\\tan45^\\circ=1$, $\\tan60^\\circ=\\sqrt3$.\n### Worked example\n**Solve $2\\cos^2x+\\sin x=1$ for $0\\le x<360^\\circ$.** Replace $\\cos^2x$ with $1-\\sin^2x$: $2-2\\sin^2x+\\sin x=1$, so $2\\sin^2x-\\sin x-1=0$. This factorises to $(2\\sin x+1)(\\sin x-1)=0$.\nSo $\\sin x=1$, giving $x=90^\\circ$, or $\\sin x=-\\tfrac12$, giving $x=210^\\circ$ or $330^\\circ$.\n### Watch out\n- Never divide by $\\sin x$ — you lose the solutions where $\\sin x=0$. Factorise instead.\n- Check every solution is inside the interval.","p1_11":"### The idea\nA vector has both size and direction, e.g. a displacement of \"3 right and 4 up\", written $3\\mathbf i+4\\mathbf j$. Add vectors nose to tail. The vector from $A$ to $B$ is \"go back to the origin, then to $B$\": $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a$.\nTwo vectors are **parallel** when one is a multiple of the other. That is how you prove three points lie on a line.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"155\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">O</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"80\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">a</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"148\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">b</text><text x=\"175\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#d97706\" stroke=\"none\">b − a</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $|x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$; the angle with $\\mathbf i$ is $\\tan^{-1}\\frac yx$ (draw a sketch).\n- Unit vector: $\\dfrac{\\mathbf a}{|\\mathbf a|}$.\n- Position vector of the midpoint of $AB$: $\\tfrac12(\\mathbf a+\\mathbf b)$.\n### Worked example\n**$\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $M$ is the midpoint of $AB$. Find $\\overrightarrow{OM}$.** $\\overrightarrow{OM}=\\mathbf a+\\tfrac12(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)=\\tfrac12\\mathbf a+\\tfrac12\\mathbf b$.\n### Watch out\n- When a question involves ratios like $AP:PB=1:2$, write each route separately, then compare coefficients.","p1_12":"### The idea\nDifferentiation finds the gradient of a curve at any point. Take a chord from $x$ to $x+h$, find its gradient, then let $h$ shrink to 0. This is **differentiation from first principles**. Doing it for $x^n$ gives the shortcut $nx^{n-1}$.\nThe derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ tells you how fast $y$ changes. Positive means increasing, negative means decreasing, and zero means a **stationary point** (the top of a hill or the bottom of a valley).\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><path d=\"M20 140 Q160 -40 300 140\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"70\" r=\"4\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"50\" r=\"4\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"90\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"70\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x+h</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#d97706\" stroke=\"none\">chord → tangent as h→0</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}$ and $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^n)=nx^{n-1}$.\n- Tangent at $x=a$: gradient $f'(a)$. Normal: gradient $-\\dfrac1{f'(a)}$.\n- At a stationary point $f'(x)=0$. If $f''>0$ it is a minimum; if $f''<0$ it is a maximum.\n### Worked example\n**Find the stationary points of $y=x^3-3x$.** $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3x^2-3=0$, so $x=\\pm1$.\n$\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=6x$. At $x=1$ this is positive, so $(1,-2)$ is a minimum. At $x=-1$ it is negative, so $(-1,2)$ is a maximum.\n**Optimisation:** an open box has a square base of side $x$ and volume 500. Its surface area is $S=x^2+\\frac{2000}{x}$. $S'=2x-\\frac{2000}{x^2}=0$ gives $x=10$, and $S''>0$ confirms this is a minimum.\n### Watch out\n- Rewrite fractions and roots as powers before differentiating.\n- Always justify whether a stationary point is a max or a min.","p1_13":"### The idea\nIntegration reverses differentiation. If $\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^2$, then $y=\\frac{x^3}{3}+c$. You need $+c$ because constants vanish when you differentiate.\nA **definite integral** $\\int_a^bf(x)\\,dx$ adds up thin strips under the curve, so it gives the area between the curve and the $x$-axis. Area below the axis counts as negative.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"120\"/><path d=\"M60 120 L60 70 Q150 10 250 50 L250 120 Z\" fill=\"#1a7f45\" fill-opacity=\"0.18\" stroke=\"none\"/><path d=\"M30 90 Q150 10 290 60\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"55\" y=\"138\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">a</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"138\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">b</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"100\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">area = ∫ f(x) dx</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $\\displaystyle\\int x^n\\,dx=\\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+c$ for $n\\neq-1$.\n- $\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx=[F(x)]_a^b=F(b)-F(a)$.\n- Area between a line and a curve: $\\int(\\text{upper}-\\text{lower})\\,dx$.\n### Worked example\n**Find the area enclosed by $y=x(4-x)$ and the $x$-axis.** The roots are 0 and 4. $\\int_0^4(4x-x^2)\\,dx=\\left[2x^2-\\frac{x^3}3\\right]_0^4=32-\\frac{64}3=\\frac{32}3$.\n### Watch out\n- If the curve crosses the axis, integrate each part separately and add the absolute values.\n- To find $f(x)$ from $f'(x)$ and a point, substitute the point to find $c$.","p1_14":"### The idea\nIn exponential growth, a quantity is multiplied by the same factor every step: $y=a^x$. The special base $e\\approx2.718$ is the one where the gradient of $e^x$ equals $e^x$ itself. That makes it the natural choice for growth and decay models.\nA **logarithm** answers \"what power?\": $\\log_2 8=3$ because $2^3=8$. Logs undo exponentials, which lets you solve for an unknown power.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"150\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"165\"/><path d=\"M20 146 Q90 140 120 110 T170 10\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M106 165 Q120 100 160 70 T290 40\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"15\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#1a7f45\" stroke=\"none\">y = eˣ</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#d97706\" stroke=\"none\">y = ln x</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $\\log_ab=c\\iff a^c=b$; $\\ln$ is $\\log_e$, so $\\ln e^x=x$ and $e^{\\ln x}=x$.\n- $\\log a+\\log b=\\log ab$, $\\log a-\\log b=\\log\\frac ab$, $\\log a^n=n\\log a$.\n- $\\dfrac{d}{dx}e^{kx}=ke^{kx}$.\n- $y=ax^n$ becomes the straight line $\\log y=\\log a+n\\log x$. $y=ab^x$ becomes $\\log y=\\log a+x\\log b$.\n### Worked example\n**Solve $3^{2x-1}=20$.** $(2x-1)\\ln3=\\ln20$, so $x=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{\\ln20}{\\ln3}+1\\right)=1.86$.\n**A car is worth $V=18000e^{-0.2t}$. When is it worth £9000?** $e^{-0.2t}=0.5$, so $t=\\frac{\\ln2}{0.2}=3.47$ years.\n### Watch out\n- $\\log(a+b)$ cannot be split up.\n- In modelling questions, comment on limitations, e.g. the value never actually reaches 0.","p2_1":"### The idea\n**Proof by contradiction**: suppose the statement is false, then show this leads to something impossible. So the statement must be true.\nThe classic example: suppose $\\sqrt2=\\frac ab$ in lowest terms. Then $a^2=2b^2$, so $a$ is even. Writing $a=2k$ gives $b^2=2k^2$, so $b$ is even too. But then $\\frac ab$ wasn't in lowest terms — a contradiction.\n**Partial fractions** reverse adding fractions. They split $\\dfrac{5x+1}{(x-1)(x+2)}$ into $\\dfrac{A}{x-1}+\\dfrac{B}{x+2}$. This makes integration and binomial expansion much easier.\n### Key results\n- Distinct linear factors: $\\dfrac{A}{x-a}+\\dfrac{B}{x-b}$.\n- Repeated factor: $\\dfrac{A}{x-a}+\\dfrac{B}{(x-a)^2}+\\dfrac{C}{x-b}$.\n- Improper fraction (top degree $\\ge$ bottom degree): divide first.\n### Worked example\n**Write $\\dfrac{5x+1}{(x-1)(x+2)}$ in partial fractions.** $5x+1=A(x+2)+B(x-1)$.\nAt $x=1$: $6=3A$, so $A=2$. At $x=-2$: $-9=-3B$, so $B=3$. The answer is $\\dfrac2{x-1}+\\dfrac3{x+2}$.\n### Watch out\n- In a contradiction proof, clearly state the assumption at the start and the contradiction at the end.\n- With a repeated factor, substituting values only finds some constants; compare coefficients for the rest.","p2_2":"### The idea\nA **function** turns each input into exactly one output. The **domain** is the set of allowed inputs and the **range** is the set of outputs you get.\n**Composite** $fg(x)$ means do $g$ first, then $f$. The **inverse** $f^{-1}$ undoes $f$. Its graph is the reflection of $f$ in $y=x$, and it only exists if $f$ is one-to-one.\nThe **modulus** $|x|$ is the distance from 0, so it is never negative. For $y=|f(x)|$, any part of the graph below the axis is flipped up.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"110\"/><path d=\"M30 20 L150 110 L270 20\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"40\" fill=\"#1a7f45\" stroke=\"none\">y = |x|</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- To find $f^{-1}$: write $y=f(x)$, swap $x$ and $y$, then rearrange for $y$.\n- The domain of $f^{-1}$ is the range of $f$ (and the other way round).\n- $|f(x)|=k$: solve $f(x)=k$ and $f(x)=-k$, then check your answers (a sketch helps).\n### Worked example\n**$f(x)=\\dfrac{3}{x-1}$ for $x>1$. Find $f^{-1}$.** $x=\\frac{3}{y-1}$, so $y=1+\\frac3x$. Hence $f^{-1}(x)=1+\\frac3x$ with domain $x>0$.\n**Solve $|2x-1|=x+2$.** $2x-1=x+2$ gives $x=3$. $2x-1=-(x+2)$ gives $x=-\\frac13$. Both check out.\n### Watch out\n- $f^{-1}(x)\\neq\\dfrac1{f(x)}$.\n- $fg(x)$ is usually different from $gf(x)$.","p2_3":"### The idea\nIn an **arithmetic** sequence you add the same amount $d$ each time. In a **geometric** sequence you multiply by the same ratio $r$.\nTo get the arithmetic sum formula, write the series forwards and backwards and add: every pair adds to (first $+$ last).\nIf $|r|<1$, the terms of a geometric series shrink so fast that the sum settles to a finite limit, the **sum to infinity**.\n### Key results\n- Arithmetic: $u_n=a+(n-1)d$, $S_n=\\frac n2(2a+(n-1)d)=\\frac n2(a+l)$.\n- Geometric: $u_n=ar^{n-1}$, $S_n=\\dfrac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}$, $S_\\infty=\\dfrac a{1-r}$ for $|r|<1$.\n- $\\sum_{r=1}^{n}$ notation; $u_{n+1}=f(u_n)$ is a recurrence relation (sequences can be increasing, decreasing or periodic).\n### Worked example\n**A geometric series has $a=8$ and $S_\\infty=12$. Find $r$.** $\\frac{8}{1-r}=12$, so $r=\\frac13$.\n**Find the least $n$ such that $S_n>1000$ for $1+2+4+\\dots$** $2^n-1>1000$, so $n>\\log_2 1001\\approx9.97$. The least $n$ is $10$.\n### Watch out\n- Use logs to find $n$; it must be a whole number, so round up.\n- With $\\sum_{r=3}^{10}$, there are $8$ terms, not $10$.","p2_4":"### The idea\nThe binomial expansion also works for negative and fractional powers. In that case it never stops — it becomes an infinite series. It only gives the correct value when the series converges, which needs $|x|<1$.\nFor $(a+bx)^n$, factor out $a^n$ first so the bracket starts with 1.\n### Key results\n- $(1+x)^n=1+nx+\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2!}x^2+\\dfrac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3!}x^3+\\dots$, valid for $|x|<1$.\n- $(a+bx)^n=a^n\\left(1+\\tfrac bax\\right)^n$, valid for $|x|<\\left|\\tfrac ab\\right|$.\n### Worked example\n**Expand $\\sqrt{4-x}$ up to $x^2$.** $\\sqrt{4-x}=2\\left(1-\\tfrac x4\\right)^{\\frac12}=2\\left(1-\\tfrac x8-\\tfrac{x^2}{128}\\right)=2-\\tfrac x4-\\tfrac{x^2}{64}$. Valid for $|x|<4$.\n### Watch out\n- Keep the sign inside: $\\left(-\\frac x4\\right)^2$ is positive.\n- When estimating with $x$, check it is within the validity range.","p2_5":"### The idea\nA **radian** is the angle where the arc length equals the radius. A full circle has arc $2\\pi r$, so it measures $2\\pi$ radians. Radians make the arc and sector formulas simple, and they are needed in calculus.\nFor small angles, the triangle and the sector almost match, which gives the approximations $\\sin\\theta\\approx\\theta$ and $\\tan\\theta\\approx\\theta$.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><path d=\"M40 150 L200 150 A160 160 0 0 0 153 37 Z\" fill=\"#1a7f45\" fill-opacity=\"0.18\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"140\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">θ</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"165\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">r</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"90\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">s = rθ</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $\\pi$ rad $=180^\\circ$. Arc $s=r\\theta$; sector area $A=\\tfrac12r^2\\theta$.\n- Segment area $=\\tfrac12r^2(\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$.\n- Small angles: $\\sin\\theta\\approx\\theta$, $\\tan\\theta\\approx\\theta$, $\\cos\\theta\\approx1-\\frac{\\theta^2}2$.\n### Worked example\n**Find an approximation for $\\dfrac{1-\\cos2\\theta}{\\theta\\sin\\theta}$ when $\\theta$ is small.** $1-\\cos2\\theta\\approx1-\\left(1-2\\theta^2\\right)=2\\theta^2$ and $\\theta\\sin\\theta\\approx\\theta^2$, so the expression is approximately $2$.\n### Watch out\n- Put your calculator in radian mode.\n- Perimeter of a sector $=2r+r\\theta$.","p2_6":"### The idea\n$\\sec$, $\\operatorname{cosec}$ and $\\cot$ are the reciprocals of $\\cos$, $\\sin$ and $\\tan$. Their graphs have asymptotes wherever the original function is zero.\nDividing $\\sin^2+\\cos^2=1$ by $\\cos^2$ or by $\\sin^2$ gives two new identities. These are the key to solving equations that mix the functions.\nInverse trig functions such as $\\arcsin$ only work on a restricted range, so that each input gives exactly one output.\n### Key results\n- $1+\\tan^2x\\equiv\\sec^2x$ and $1+\\cot^2x\\equiv\\operatorname{cosec}^2x$.\n- $\\arcsin$ has range $\\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$, $\\arccos$ has range $[0,\\pi]$, and $\\arctan$ has range $\\left(-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right)$.\n### Worked example\n**Solve $2\\tan^2x+\\sec x=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi$.** Write $\\tan^2x=\\sec^2x-1$: $2\\sec^2x+\\sec x-3=0$, so $(2\\sec x+3)(\\sec x-1)=0$.\n$\\sec x=1$ gives $x=0$. $\\cos x=-\\tfrac23$ gives $x=2.30$ or $3.98$.\n### Watch out\n- $\\sec x$ is never between $-1$ and $1$.","p2_7":"### The idea\nThe **addition formulae** expand trig functions of a sum, such as $\\sin(A+B)$. Setting $B=A$ gives the **double-angle formulae**.\nAny wave of the form $a\\sin x+b\\cos x$ is really a single sine wave that has been shifted. Writing it as $R\\sin(x+\\alpha)$ shows its maximum ($R$) straight away and makes the equation easy to solve.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"75\"/><path d=\"M10 75 C45 15 85 15 120 75 S195 135 230 75 S305 15 340 75\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"25\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/><text x=\"240\" y=\"20\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">max = R</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $\\sin(A\\pm B)=\\sin A\\cos B\\pm\\cos A\\sin B$; $\\cos(A\\pm B)=\\cos A\\cos B\\mp\\sin A\\sin B$.\n- $\\sin2A=2\\sin A\\cos A$; $\\cos2A=2\\cos^2A-1=1-2\\sin^2A$; $\\tan2A=\\dfrac{2\\tan A}{1-\\tan^2A}$.\n- $a\\sin x+b\\cos x=R\\sin(x+\\alpha)$, with $R\\cos\\alpha=a$, $R\\sin\\alpha=b$, $R=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$.\n### Worked example\n**Write $3\\sin x+4\\cos x$ as $R\\sin(x+\\alpha)$ and give its maximum.** $R=5$ and $\\tan\\alpha=\\frac43$, so $\\alpha=0.927$. The maximum is $5$, when $x+0.927=\\frac\\pi2$, i.e. $x=0.644$.\n### Watch out\n- Match the form exactly: $R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$ gives different equations for $\\alpha$.\n- When proving an identity, start from one side and work through to the other.","p2_8":"### The idea\n**Parametric equations** give $x$ and $y$ separately in terms of a third variable $t$, like a position at time $t$. To find the Cartesian equation, eliminate $t$: rearrange one equation for $t$, or use a trig identity.\n### Key results\n- If $x=a\\cos t$ and $y=a\\sin t$, then $x^2+y^2=a^2$ (a circle).\n- The range of $t$ restricts which part of the curve you get.\n- Intersections: substitute $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ into the other equation and solve for $t$.\n### Worked example\n**$x=2t-1$, $y=t^2+3$. Find the Cartesian equation.** $t=\\frac{x+1}{2}$, so $y=\\frac{(x+1)^2}{4}+3$.\n**$x=\\sin t$, $y=\\cos2t$.** $\\cos2t=1-2\\sin^2t$, so $y=1-2x^2$ for $-1\\le x\\le1$.\n### Watch out\n- State the domain and range that come from the limits on $t$.","p2_9":"### The idea\n**Chain rule**: for a function of a function, differentiate the outside and multiply by the derivative of the inside.\n**Product rule** for $uv$ and **quotient rule** for $\\frac uv$.\n**Implicit differentiation**: differentiate both sides with respect to $x$. Whenever you differentiate a $y$ term, multiply by $\\frac{dy}{dx}$, because $y$ depends on $x$.\n**Parametric**: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}$.\nThe second derivative shows how the gradient changes, which tells you whether the curve is concave or convex.\n### Key results\n- $(\\sin x)'=\\cos x$, $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x$, $(\\tan x)'=\\sec^2x$, $(e^x)'=e^x$, $(\\ln x)'=\\frac1x$, $(a^x)'=a^x\\ln a$.\n- $(uv)'=u'v+uv'$ and $\\left(\\frac uv\\right)'=\\dfrac{u'v-uv'}{v^2}$.\n- A point of inflection is where $f''(x)$ changes sign.\n- Connected rates: $\\frac{dV}{dt}=\\frac{dV}{dr}\\cdot\\frac{dr}{dt}$.\n### Worked example\n**Differentiate $y=x^2e^{3x}$.** $y'=2xe^{3x}+3x^2e^{3x}=xe^{3x}(2+3x)$.\n**Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $x^2+xy+y^2=7$.** $2x+y+x\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$, so $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{2x+y}{x+2y}$.\n**A sphere's volume grows at $10$ cm³/s. How fast is $r$ growing when $r=5$?** $\\frac{dV}{dr}=4\\pi r^2=100\\pi$, so $\\frac{dr}{dt}=\\frac{10}{100\\pi}=0.0318$ cm/s.\n### Watch out\n- In implicit differentiation, $xy$ needs the product rule.","p2_10":"### The idea\nMost equations can't be solved exactly, so we approximate.\nIf $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ have opposite signs and $f$ is continuous, the graph must cross zero between $a$ and $b$.\n**Iteration** rearranges $f(x)=0$ into $x=g(x)$, then repeats $x_{n+1}=g(x_n)$. Depending on the rearrangement, it converges (staircase or cobweb diagram) or diverges.\n**Newton–Raphson** follows the tangent down to the axis, which usually converges very quickly.\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"130\"/><path d=\"M40 150 Q150 120 260 10\" stroke=\"#1a7f45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"250\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"22\" r=\"4\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"145\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x₀</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#d97706\" stroke=\"none\">x₁</text></svg>\n### Key results\n- $x_{n+1}=x_n-\\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}$.\n- To show a root is $2.35$ to 2 d.p., show there is a sign change between $2.345$ and $2.355$.\n- Newton–Raphson fails near a stationary point, where $f'\\approx0$.\n### Worked example\n**$f(x)=x^3-2x-5$, $x_0=2$.** $f(2)=-1$ and $f'(2)=10$, so $x_1=2+0.1=2.1$.\n### Watch out\n- A sign change can also happen at an asymptote, so check the function is continuous.","p2_11":"### The idea\nTo integrate, recognise a standard result or change the integral into one.\n- **Reverse chain rule:** spot a function together with its derivative.\n- **Substitution:** replace a messy part with $u$. Change $dx$ to $du$, and change the limits too.\n- **Integration by parts:** use it for products. Pick $u$ to be the part that gets simpler when differentiated (LATE: logs, algebra, trig, exponentials).\n- **Differential equations:** separate the variables, then integrate both sides.\n### Key results\n- $\\int e^{kx}dx=\\frac1ke^{kx}$, $\\int\\frac1x\\,dx=\\ln|x|$, $\\int\\sin kx\\,dx=-\\frac1k\\cos kx$, $\\int\\sec^2x\\,dx=\\tan x$.\n- $\\int\\dfrac{f'(x)}{f(x)}dx=\\ln|f(x)|+c$.\n- $\\int u\\,v'\\,dx=uv-\\int v\\,u'\\,dx$.\n- Trapezium rule: $\\frac h2[y_0+2(y_1+\\dots+y_{n-1})+y_n]$.\n- Use identities such as $\\sin^2x=\\frac12(1-\\cos2x)$.\n### Worked example\n**$\\int x\\cos x\\,dx$.** Let $u=x$ and $v'=\\cos x$: $x\\sin x-\\int\\sin x\\,dx=x\\sin x+\\cos x+c$.\n**Solve $\\frac{dy}{dx}=xy$, with $y=2$ when $x=0$.** $\\int\\frac1y\\,dy=\\int x\\,dx$, so $\\ln y=\\frac{x^2}2+c$. Hence $y=2e^{x^2/2}$.\n### Watch out\n- Don't leave $x$ inside an integral in $u$.","p2_12":"### The idea\n3D vectors work exactly like 2D ones, with a third component $\\mathbf k$. Distances use Pythagoras in three dimensions. The angle a vector makes with an axis comes from comparing that component with the vector's length.\n### Key results\n- $|x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j+z\\mathbf k|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$.\n- Angle with the $x$-axis: $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{x}{|\\mathbf a|}$.\n- $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a$. Collinear points: $\\overrightarrow{AB}=k\\overrightarrow{BC}$.\n### Worked example\n**$A(1,2,3)$ and $B(4,6,3)$. Find $|AB|$.** $\\overrightarrow{AB}=3\\mathbf i+4\\mathbf j$, so $|AB|=5$.\n### Watch out\n- Show every component when you subtract vectors."},"fs1":{"drv":["$E(X)=\\sum xP(X=x)$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E(X^2)-[E(X)]^2$.","$E(aX+b)=aE(X)+b$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)$.","$E(g(X))=\\sum g(x)P(X=x)$.","Discrete uniform on $1..n$: mean $\\tfrac{n+1}2$, variance $\\tfrac{n^2-1}{12}$."],"poisson":["$X\\sim Po(\\lambda)$: events occur singly, independently and at a constant average rate.","$P(X=r)=\\dfrac{e^{-\\lambda}\\lambda^r}{r!}$; mean $=$ variance $=\\lambda$.","Scale $\\lambda$ with the interval length. Sum of independent Poissons: $Po(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$.","$B(n,p)$ can be approximated by $Po(np)$ when $n$ is large and $p$ is small."],"geomnb":["Geometric (trials until the first success): $P(X=x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p$, mean $\\tfrac1p$, variance $\\tfrac{1-p}{p^2}$.","$P(X>x)=(1-p)^x$.","Negative binomial (trials until the $r$th success): $P(X=x)=\\binom{x-1}{r-1}p^r(1-p)^{x-r}$, mean $\\tfrac rp$, variance $\\tfrac{r(1-p)}{p^2}$."],"hyp":["Tests for Poisson and geometric parameters work like binomial tests: find the critical region or a p-value.","Use a normal approximation to Poisson ($N(\\lambda,\\lambda)$) or binomial when the parameters are large, with a continuity correction.","State the hypotheses in terms of the parameter, and conclude in context."],"clt":["Central limit theorem: for large $n$, $\\bar X\\approx N\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$ whatever the distribution of $X$.","If $X$ is normal, then $\\bar X$ is exactly normal for any $n$.","Use it for probabilities about sample means or sums ($\\sum X\\approx N(n\\mu,n\\sigma^2)$)."],"chi":["$\\chi^2=\\sum\\dfrac{(O-E)^2}{E}$ (or $\\sum\\frac{O^2}E-N$).","Combine classes so that every expected frequency is $\\ge5$.","Degrees of freedom $=$ number of classes $-$ 1 $-$ number of parameters estimated. Contingency tables: $(r-1)(c-1)$.","Goodness of fit: uniform, binomial, Poisson, geometric and so on. Independence: contingency tables.","Reject $H_0$ if $\\chi^2>$ the critical value."],"pgf":["$G_X(t)=E(t^X)=\\sum P(X=x)t^x$, with $G_X(1)=1$.","$E(X)=G'(1)$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=G''(1)+G'(1)-[G'(1)]^2$.","Sum of independent variables: $G_{X+Y}(t)=G_X(t)G_Y(t)$. Also $G_{aX+b}(t)=t^bG_X(t^a)$.","Know the standard PGFs: binomial, Poisson, geometric, negative binomial."],"quality":["Type I error: rejecting a true $H_0$. Its probability is the actual significance level.","Type II error: accepting a false $H_0$. $P=P(\\text{not in CR}\\mid H_1$ value$)$.","Power $=1-P(\\text{Type II})$; a power function plots power against the parameter.","Choosing a test: compare power — the more powerful test is better."]},"fs2":{"linreg":["Least squares line: $b=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}$, $a=\\bar y-b\\bar x$.","Residual $=$ observed $-$ predicted. Residual sum of squares $=S_{yy}-\\dfrac{S_{xy}^2}{S_{xx}}$.","A residual plot with a random scatter means a linear model fits; a pattern means it doesn't.","Coding: convert back carefully."],"corr":["PMCC $r=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx}S_{yy}}}$. Test $\\rho=0$ with the tables; assumes bivariate normal.","Spearman's rank $r_s=1-\\dfrac{6\\sum d^2}{n(n^2-1)}$; use it for ranked data or non-linear monotonic relationships.","Tied ranks: give the average rank, then use the PMCC formula on the ranks.","Conclude in context: correlation or association."],"cont":["PDF $f(x)\\ge0$ with $\\int f=1$. CDF $F(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^xf(t)\\,dt$, and $f=F'$.","$E(X)=\\int xf(x)\\,dx$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\int x^2f(x)\\,dx-\\mu^2$.","Median: $F(m)=0.5$. Quartiles and percentiles similarly. Mode: maximum of $f$.","Continuous uniform on $[a,b]$: mean $\\tfrac{a+b}2$, variance $\\tfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}$."],"combo":["$E(aX\\pm bY)=aE(X)\\pm bE(Y)$.","If independent: $\\operatorname{Var}(aX\\pm bY)=a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)+b^2\\operatorname{Var}(Y)$ — always add the variances.","Linear combinations of independent normals are normal.","$X_1+X_2$ (two separate items) differs from $2X$ (one item doubled): the variances are $2\\sigma^2$ and $4\\sigma^2$."],"estim":["Unbiased estimators: $\\bar x$ for $\\mu$, $s^2=\\dfrac{S_{xx}}{n-1}$ for $\\sigma^2$. Bias $=E(\\hat\\theta)-\\theta$.","A better estimator has a smaller variance.","Confidence interval for $\\mu$ with known $\\sigma$: $\\bar x\\pm z\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}$.","Difference of means test: $Z=\\dfrac{(\\bar x-\\bar y)-(\\mu_x-\\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x}+\\frac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}}$."],"furtherhyp":["Variance test: $\\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2}\\sim\\chi^2_{n-1}$, assuming a normal population.","F-test for equal variances: $F=\\dfrac{s_1^2}{s_2^2}$ (larger on top) with $F_{n_1-1,n_2-1}$.","Confidence interval for $\\sigma^2$ uses the $\\chi^2$ tables.","State the assumptions: normality and independent samples."],"tdist":["With unknown $\\sigma$ and a small normal sample: $T=\\dfrac{\\bar X-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\\sim t_{n-1}$.","Confidence interval: $\\bar x\\pm t\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt n}$.","Paired $t$-test on the differences $d$.","Two-sample $t$-test uses the pooled variance $s_p^2=\\dfrac{(n_1-1)s_1^2+(n_2-1)s_2^2}{n_1+n_2-2}$ and assumes equal variances."]},"fm1":{"momentum":["Momentum $=mv$ (a vector). Impulse $=Ft=mv-mu$, measured in N s.","Conservation of linear momentum in collisions with no external impulse.","Vector impulse: $\\mathbf I=m\\mathbf v-m\\mathbf u$.","Draw before and after diagrams and choose a positive direction."],"wep":["Work done $=Fd$ (in the direction of motion). KE $=\\tfrac12mv^2$; GPE $=mgh$.","Work–energy principle: work done by non-gravity forces $=$ change in mechanical energy.","Work done against friction $=\\mu Rd$ (with $R$ from resolving).","Power $P=Fv$; at maximum (constant) speed the driving force equals the resistance."],"elastic":["Hooke's law: $T=\\dfrac{\\lambda x}{l}$ ($\\lambda$ is the modulus of elasticity, $l$ the natural length).","Elastic potential energy $=\\dfrac{\\lambda x^2}{2l}$.","Strings only exert tension when stretched; springs can also be compressed.","Use energy conservation including EPE; equilibrium is where the tension balances the weight."],"coll1d":["Newton's law of restitution: speed of separation $=e\\times$ speed of approach, $0\\le e\\le1$.","Combine with conservation of momentum to find the velocities after the collision.","Rebound from a wall: $v=eu$.","KE loss $=$ KE before $-$ KE after; it is $0$ only if $e=1$.","Successive collisions: check whether another collision happens by comparing velocities."],"coll2d":["Smooth sphere hitting a wall: the velocity component parallel to the wall is unchanged; the perpendicular component becomes $e\\times$ itself, reversed.","Two spheres: momentum along the line of centres is conserved and restitution applies along it; the perpendicular components are unchanged.","Use vectors $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j$ and components along the line of centres.","Angle of deflection: use $\\tan$ of the components, or the scalar product."]},"fm2":{"circ":["Angular speed $\\omega$; $v=r\\omega$; acceleration towards the centre $=r\\omega^2=\\dfrac{v^2}r$.","Horizontal circles (conical pendulum, banked tracks): resolve vertically for equilibrium and horizontally for $F=mr\\omega^2$.","Vertical circles: energy conservation plus $F=\\dfrac{mv^2}{r}$ at a point.","A string goes slack when $T=0$; a particle leaves a surface when $R=0$. A rod can push, so it can have $v=0$ at the top."],"com1":["System of particles: $\\bar x=\\dfrac{\\sum m_ix_i}{\\sum m_i}$.","Laminas: use the symmetry and standard results (formula book: sector, semicircle, triangle at $\\tfrac23$ of the median).","Composite laminas: add or subtract with a table of mass and position.","Suspended freely: the centre of mass is vertically below the point of suspension, so find the angle with $\\tan$."],"com2":["Uniform plane regions by integration: $\\bar x=\\dfrac{\\int xy\\,dx}{\\int y\\,dx}$ and $\\bar y=\\dfrac{\\tfrac12\\int y^2\\,dx}{\\int y\\,dx}$.","Solids of revolution: $\\bar x=\\dfrac{\\int\\pi xy^2\\,dx}{\\int\\pi y^2\\,dx}$.","Standard solids: hemisphere $\\tfrac38r$ and cone $\\tfrac14h$ from the base.","Toppling or sliding on a plane: the object topples when its centre of mass is beyond the edge of the base."],"kin":["Variable acceleration as a function of $t$: integrate or differentiate.","As a function of $x$: $a=v\\dfrac{dv}{dx}=\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\tfrac12v^2\\right)$.","As a function of $v$: separate the variables using $a=\\dfrac{dv}{dt}$ or $v\\dfrac{dv}{dx}$.","Use the initial conditions to find the constants."],"dyn":["$F=ma$ with variable forces, e.g. resistance $kv$ or $kv^2$, leads to a differential equation.","Choose $m\\dfrac{dv}{dt}$ (when $t$ is involved) or $mv\\dfrac{dv}{dx}$ (when distance is involved).","Terminal velocity: when $a=0$.","Integrate with limits to avoid finding $+c$."]},"d1":{"algo":["Follow an algorithm exactly, tracing it in a table.","Bubble sort: compare adjacent items; at most $n-1$ passes. Quick sort: choose a pivot (the middle item) and split into sublists.","Bin packing: first-fit, first-fit decreasing and full bin. Lower bound $=\\left\\lceil\\dfrac{\\text{total}}{\\text{bin size}}\\right\\rceil$.","Order: e.g. $O(n^2)$ means time scales with $n^2$ — scale up proportionally."],"graphs":["Vocabulary: vertex/node, edge/arc, degree (valency), path, cycle, connected, tree, spanning tree, simple graph.","Sum of degrees $=2\\times$ number of edges.","Complete graph $K_n$ has $\\tfrac12n(n-1)$ edges. Bipartite $K_{m,n}$.","Eulerian: all vertices even. Semi-Eulerian: exactly two odd vertices.","Planar graphs: the planarity algorithm, and Euler's formula $V+R=E+2$."],"algraph":["Kruskal: add edges in order of weight, avoiding cycles.","Prim: grow the tree from a vertex, adding the nearest vertex each time. The matrix form works the same way.","Dijkstra: find the shortest path; working values and order of labelling must be shown.","Floyd's algorithm: distance and route matrices, updated through each vertex in turn."],"route":["Route inspection (Chinese postman): every edge at least once, returning to the start.","All vertices even: route length $=$ total edge weight.","Odd vertices: pair them up to minimise the extra repeated distance, and add it.","If the start and end can differ, leave the \"worst\" pair of odd vertices unpaired."],"tsp":["Classical (each vertex exactly once) versus practical (each vertex at least once). Create a table of least distances.","Upper bound: nearest neighbour algorithm (or a doubled minimum spanning tree plus shortcuts). Lowest upper bound is best.","Lower bound: delete a vertex, find the MST of the rest, then add the two shortest edges to the deleted vertex. The highest lower bound is best.","The optimal tour lies between the best lower and upper bounds."],"cpa":["Activity networks: activity on arc; dummies show dependencies or keep activities unique.","Early event times on the forward pass (take the max); late times on the backward pass (take the min).","Critical activities have total float $0$. Float $=$ late finish $-$ duration $-$ early start.","Gantt (cascade) charts, resource histograms, and the lower bound on workers $=\\left\\lceil\\dfrac{\\text{total activity time}}{\\text{critical path length}}\\right\\rceil$."],"lp":["Formulate: decision variables, an objective function (maximise or minimise), constraints including non-negativity.","Graphical method: draw the constraints and shade the unwanted regions; the feasible region is left unshaded.","The optimum is at a vertex: use the objective line (\"ruler\") method or test the vertices.","Integer solutions: check nearby integer points."],"simplex":["Slack variables turn $\\le$ constraints into equations. Set up the initial tableau.","Pivot column: most negative value in the objective row. Pivot row: smallest positive $\\theta$ ratio.","Optimal when the objective row has no negatives (for maximising).","$\\ge$ constraints: surplus and artificial variables with the two-stage method or big-M."]},"d2":{"transport":["Balance supply and demand with a dummy if needed. Find an initial solution by the north-west corner method.","Degeneracy: a solution needs $m+n-1$ occupied cells; add a zero if fewer.","Shadow costs give improvement indices; enter the most negative index, then use the stepping-stone method.","Optimal when all improvement indices are $\\ge0$. Can be formulated as an LP."],"alloc":["Hungarian algorithm: reduce rows, then columns. Cover the zeros with the minimum number of lines; if fewer than $n$, augment.","Maximise: subtract every value from the largest first.","Unequal numbers: add a dummy row or column. Impossible pairings: use a very large cost.","Formulate as an LP with $x_{ij}\\in\\{0,1\\}$."],"flows":["Cut capacity: sum of the capacities of arcs from the source side to the sink side.","Max-flow min-cut theorem: max flow $=$ min cut.","Labelling procedure: find flow-augmenting routes until none remain.","Multiple sources or sinks: add a supersource or supersink. Vertex capacities: split the vertex.","Lower capacities: check feasibility."],"dp":["Bellman's principle of optimality: work backwards through the stages.","Table columns: stage, state, action, destination, value.","Problem types: minimax, maximin, shortest/longest path, production planning.","Read the optimal route forwards from the start state."],"game":["Play-safe strategies: row player maximin, column player minimax. A stable solution (saddle point) exists if they are equal.","Dominance: remove dominated rows or columns.","$2\\times n$ games: graphical method for the optimal mixed strategy.","Larger games: convert to an LP (add a constant so all values are positive) and use simplex.","Zero-sum: the column player's value is the negative of the row player's."],"recur":["First order $u_{n+1}=au_n+b$: solution $=$ CF $+$ PS.","Second order: auxiliary equation; repeated root $(A+Bn)\\lambda^n$; complex roots give oscillation.","Use the initial terms to find the constants.","Model populations, loans and interest in context."],"decision":["Decision trees: decision nodes (squares), chance nodes (circles), payoffs at the ends.","Roll back: at chance nodes take the expected monetary value (EMV), at decision nodes take the best option.","EMV assumes the decision is repeated; real decision-makers may be risk-averse.","Utility functions can replace money values."]},"cp1":{"complex":["$i^2=-1$; $z=x+iy$; conjugate $z^*=x-iy$; $zz^*=|z|^2$.","Divide by multiplying the top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator.","Real polynomials have complex roots in conjugate pairs.","Equate real and imaginary parts to solve equations such as $z^2=3+4i$."],"argand":["$|z|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$; $\\arg z$ is measured from the positive real axis, $-\\pi<\\arg z\\le\\pi$ (check the quadrant).","Modulus–argument form: $z=r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)$. Multiply: multiply moduli and add arguments. Divide: divide moduli and subtract arguments.","Loci: $|z-a|=r$ is a circle; $|z-a|=|z-b|$ is the perpendicular bisector; $\\arg(z-a)=\\theta$ is a half-line from $a$.","Regions: shade carefully; the half-line excludes its end point."],"series":["$\\sum r=\\tfrac12n(n+1)$, $\\sum r^2=\\tfrac16n(n+1)(2n+1)$, $\\sum r^3=\\tfrac14n^2(n+1)^2$.","$\\sum_{r=k}^{n}f(r)=\\sum_{1}^{n}-\\sum_{1}^{k-1}$.","Split sums: $\\sum(ar^2+br)=a\\sum r^2+b\\sum r$; $\\sum c=cn$.","Factorise the final answer as the question asks."],"roots":["Quadratic: $\\alpha+\\beta=-\\tfrac ba$, $\\alpha\\beta=\\tfrac ca$.","Cubic: $\\sum\\alpha=-\\tfrac ba$, $\\sum\\alpha\\beta=\\tfrac ca$, $\\alpha\\beta\\gamma=-\\tfrac da$. Quartic: the pattern continues with alternating signs.","$\\alpha^2+\\beta^2=(\\alpha+\\beta)^2-2\\alpha\\beta$; $\\sum\\alpha^2=(\\sum\\alpha)^2-2\\sum\\alpha\\beta$.","Transformed roots, e.g. $2\\alpha+1$: substitute $w=2x+1$, so $x=\\tfrac{w-1}2$, into the original equation."],"volrev":["About the $x$-axis: $V=\\pi\\int_a^b y^2\\,dx$. About the $y$-axis: $V=\\pi\\int_c^d x^2\\,dy$.","Parametric: $V=\\pi\\int y^2\\dfrac{dx}{dt}\\,dt$.","Composite solids: add or subtract volumes, e.g. a cone (formula) from a revolved curve.","Modelling: convert units and give limitations."],"matrices":["Multiply row by column. Order matters: $AB\\neq BA$ in general.","$2\\times2$: $\\det=ad-bc$, $A^{-1}=\\dfrac1{\\det}\\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\\\-c&a\\end{pmatrix}$. Singular if $\\det=0$.","$3\\times3$: determinant by cofactor expansion; inverse is $\\frac{1}{\\det}\\times$ the transpose of the cofactor matrix (often by calculator).","$(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}$.","Simultaneous equations $A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf b$: unique solution iff $\\det A\\neq0$. Otherwise the planes are consistent (sheaf) or inconsistent (prism, parallel)."],"transf":["The columns of the matrix are the images of $\\binom10$ and $\\binom01$.","Rotation by $\\theta$ anticlockwise: $\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta&-\\sin\\theta\\\\\\sin\\theta&\\cos\\theta\\end{pmatrix}$. Reflection in $y=x\\tan\\theta$: $\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos2\\theta&\\sin2\\theta\\\\\\sin2\\theta&-\\cos2\\theta\\end{pmatrix}$.","$|\\det|$ is the area (or volume) scale factor; a negative det means orientation is reversed.","$BA$ means $A$ is applied first.","Invariant points: $M\\mathbf x=\\mathbf x$. Invariant line $y=mx+c$: the image of $(x,mx+c)$ lies on the same line. A line of invariant points is different from an invariant line."],"induction":["Basis: show the statement is true for $n=1$.","Assumption: assume it is true for $n=k$.","Inductive step: show it is true for $n=k+1$ using the assumption.","Conclusion: \"true for $n=1$; if true for $n=k$ then true for $n=k+1$, so true for all $n\\in\\mathbb Z^+$.\"","Types: sums, divisibility (consider $f(k+1)-f(k)$), and matrix powers."],"vectors":["Line: $\\mathbf r=\\mathbf a+\\lambda\\mathbf b$. Cartesian form: $\\dfrac{x-a_1}{b_1}=\\dfrac{y-a_2}{b_2}=\\dfrac{z-a_3}{b_3}$.","Plane: $\\mathbf r\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf n$, i.e. $ax+by+cz=d$. Vector form $\\mathbf r=\\mathbf a+\\lambda\\mathbf b+\\mu\\mathbf c$.","Scalar product $\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=|\\mathbf a||\\mathbf b|\\cos\\theta$; perpendicular iff $\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=0$.","Angles: between lines use the directions; between a line and a plane use $\\sin\\theta=\\dfrac{|\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf n|}{|\\mathbf b||\\mathbf n|}$; between planes use the normals.","Lines can intersect, be parallel or be skew. Distance from a point to a plane: $\\dfrac{|ax_1+by_1+cz_1-d|}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2+c^2}}$. Shortest distances use perpendiculars."]},"cp2":{"complex":["Exponential form $z=re^{i\\theta}$.","De Moivre: $(r(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta))^n=r^n(\\cos n\\theta+i\\sin n\\theta)$.","Multiple angles: expand $(\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta)^n$ binomially and compare real and imaginary parts. Use $z^n+z^{-n}=2\\cos n\\theta$ and $z^n-z^{-n}=2i\\sin n\\theta$ for powers of $\\cos$ and $\\sin$.","$n$th roots of $w$: $n$ roots with equal spacing $\\tfrac{2\\pi}n$ on a circle. The roots of unity sum to $0$.","Series: write $C+iS$ as a geometric series in $e^{i\\theta}$."],"series":["Method of differences: write $u_r=f(r)-f(r+1)$, often via partial fractions, then cancel terms.","Maclaurin: $f(x)=f(0)+f'(0)x+\\dfrac{f''(0)}{2!}x^2+\\dots$","Standard series (formula book): $e^x$, $\\ln(1+x)$ (valid for $-1<x\\le1$), $\\sin x$, $\\cos x$, $(1+x)^n$.","Compound series: substitute or multiply known series, e.g. $e^{2x}\\sin x$."],"calc":["Improper integrals: replace the infinite limit with $t$ and take $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}$; the integral converges if the limit exists.","Mean value of $f$ on $[a,b]$: $\\dfrac1{b-a}\\int_a^bf(x)\\,dx$.","Inverse trig: $\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{a^2-x^2}}dx=\\arcsin\\tfrac xa+c$ and $\\int\\dfrac{1}{a^2+x^2}dx=\\tfrac1a\\arctan\\tfrac xa+c$.","Partial fractions with quadratic factors: $\\dfrac{Ax+B}{x^2+a^2}$ integrates to a log term plus an arctan term."],"volrev":["About the $y$-axis: $V=\\pi\\int x^2\\,dy$. With parametric curves, change the variable using $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ or $\\frac{dy}{dt}$.","Region between two curves: $\\pi\\int(y_1^2-y_2^2)\\,dx$ (a washer).","Modelling questions: units, scale factors and a sensible limitation."],"polar":["$x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, $r^2=x^2+y^2$.","Sketch curves such as $r=a$, $r=a\\theta$, $r=a(1\\pm\\cos\\theta)$ and $r=a\\cos2\\theta$; $r\\ge0$ only.","Area $=\\tfrac12\\int_\\alpha^\\beta r^2\\,d\\theta$.","Tangents parallel to the initial line: $\\dfrac{d(r\\sin\\theta)}{d\\theta}=0$. Perpendicular to it: $\\dfrac{d(r\\cos\\theta)}{d\\theta}=0$."],"hyper":["$\\sinh x=\\tfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}2$, $\\cosh x=\\tfrac{e^x+e^{-x}}2$, $\\tanh x=\\tfrac{\\sinh x}{\\cosh x}$.","$\\cosh^2x-\\sinh^2x=1$ (Osborn's rule: swap the sign of any product of two sinh terms).","Inverses: $\\operatorname{arsinh}x=\\ln(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1})$, $\\operatorname{arcosh}x=\\ln(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1})$ for $x\\ge1$, $\\operatorname{artanh}x=\\tfrac12\\ln\\tfrac{1+x}{1-x}$.","$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\sinh x=\\cosh x$ and $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\cosh x=\\sinh x$.","$\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+a^2}}dx=\\operatorname{arsinh}\\tfrac xa+c$ and $\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-a^2}}dx=\\operatorname{arcosh}\\tfrac xa+c$."],"de":["First order linear $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}+P(x)y=Q(x)$: integrating factor $e^{\\int P\\,dx}$, then $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(y\\,\\mathrm{IF})=Q\\,\\mathrm{IF}$.","Second order $ay''+by'+cy=0$: auxiliary equation $am^2+bm+c=0$.","Real distinct roots: $Ae^{m_1x}+Be^{m_2x}$. Repeated: $(A+Bx)e^{mx}$. Complex $p\\pm qi$: $e^{px}(A\\cos qx+B\\sin qx)$.","Non-homogeneous: general solution $=$ complementary function $+$ particular integral. Choose a PI of the same form as $f(x)$, multiplying by $x$ if it clashes with the CF."],"model":["Simple harmonic motion: $\\ddot x=-\\omega^2x$, so $x=A\\cos\\omega t+B\\sin\\omega t$.","Damping: $\\ddot x+k\\dot x+\\omega^2x=0$. Heavy damping: real roots. Critical: repeated roots. Light: complex roots (oscillations decay).","Forced oscillations: add a particular integral.","Coupled first-order systems: differentiate one equation and substitute to get a second-order DE in one variable.","Comment on the model's validity, e.g. as $t\\to\\infty$."]},"fp1":{"vectors":["Vector product $\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\a_1&a_2&a_3\\\\b_1&b_2&b_3\\end{vmatrix}$; $|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|=|\\mathbf a||\\mathbf b|\\sin\\theta$. It is perpendicular to both vectors.","Triangle area $=\\tfrac12|\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}|$; parallelogram area $=|\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b|$.","Scalar triple product: tetrahedron volume $=\\tfrac16|\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)|$; parallelepiped volume $=|\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)|$.","Line in the form $(\\mathbf r-\\mathbf a)\\times\\mathbf b=\\mathbf0$. Shortest distance between skew lines: $\\dfrac{|(\\mathbf a-\\mathbf c)\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf d)|}{|\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf d|}$."],"conic1":["Parabola $y^2=4ax$: parametric $(at^2,2at)$, focus $(a,0)$, directrix $x=-a$.","Rectangular hyperbola $xy=c^2$: parametric $\\left(ct,\\tfrac ct\\right)$.","Tangents and normals: use parametric or implicit differentiation.","Focus–directrix property: distance to the focus $=$ distance to the directrix (for the parabola).","Loci: find the coordinates of a point in terms of $t$, then eliminate $t$."],"conic2":["Ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1$: parametric $(a\\cos\\theta,b\\sin\\theta)$; $b^2=a^2(1-e^2)$; foci $(\\pm ae,0)$; directrices $x=\\pm\\tfrac ae$.","Hyperbola $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1$: parametric $(a\\cosh t,b\\sinh t)$ or $(a\\sec\\theta,b\\tan\\theta)$; $b^2=a^2(e^2-1)$; asymptotes $y=\\pm\\tfrac bax$.","Eccentricity: $e<1$ ellipse, $e=1$ parabola, $e>1$ hyperbola.","A line is a tangent when substituting gives a repeated root ($b^2-4ac=0$)."],"ineq":["Don't multiply by an expression that could be negative: multiply by its square, or rearrange to $\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}>0$.","Find the critical values (zeros of the numerator and the denominator), then test the sign on each interval.","Modulus inequalities: sketch both graphs and find the intersections by considering cases.","Exclude the values that make the denominator zero."],"tform":["$t=\\tan\\frac x2$: $\\sin x=\\dfrac{2t}{1+t^2}$, $\\cos x=\\dfrac{1-t^2}{1+t^2}$, $\\tan x=\\dfrac{2t}{1-t^2}$.","Use it to prove identities and solve equations of the form $a\\sin x+b\\cos x=c$.","When solving, check $x=\\pi$ separately (where $t$ is undefined).","Integration: $dx=\\dfrac{2\\,dt}{1+t^2}$."],"taylor":["Taylor series about $x=a$: $f(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+\\dfrac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2+\\dots$","Use it to approximate values near $a$ when the Maclaurin series doesn't converge or is inaccurate there.","Series solutions of differential equations: differentiate the DE repeatedly to get $y''(a)$, $y'''(a)$, …","Limits: use series expansions, or L'Hospital's rule $\\lim\\frac fg=\\lim\\frac{f'}{g'}$ for $\\frac00$."],"calc":["Leibniz's theorem: $\\dfrac{d^n}{dx^n}(uv)=\\sum\\binom nr u^{(n-r)}v^{(r)}$.","L'Hospital's rule for $\\frac00$ or $\\frac\\infty\\infty$ limits.","Weierstrass substitution $t=\\tan\\frac x2$ for integrals in $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$.","Standard limits: $x^ke^{-x}\\to0$ and $x^k\\ln x\\to0$ as $x\\to0^+$."],"num":["Mid-ordinate rule and Simpson's rule (even number of strips): $\\tfrac h3[y_0+4(\\text{odds})+2(\\text{evens})+y_n]$.","Euler's method: $y_{n+1}=y_n+h\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)_n$.","Second-order approximations: $\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)_n\\approx\\dfrac{y_{n+1}-y_{n-1}}{2h}$ and $\\left(\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\right)_n\\approx\\dfrac{y_{n+1}-2y_n+y_{n-1}}{h^2}$.","Accuracy improves as $h$ gets smaller; comment on the error in context."],"rde":["Reducible DEs: a given substitution (e.g. $y=vx$ or $z=y^{-1}$) turns the DE into a standard form.","Differentiate the substitution carefully (product or chain rule) to rewrite $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$.","Then solve with an integrating factor or the auxiliary equation, and substitute back.","$x=e^u$ turns an Euler-type equation $x^2y''+axy'+by=0$ into constant coefficients."]},"fp2":{"numth":["Euclid's algorithm finds $\\gcd(a,b)$; work backwards for $ax+by=\\gcd$ (Bézout).","Modular arithmetic: $a\\equiv b\\pmod n$ iff $n\\mid(a-b)$.","Fermat's little theorem: $a^{p-1}\\equiv1\\pmod p$ for prime $p$ with $p\\nmid a$.","Divisibility tests for 3, 9 and 11 (alternating digit sum).","Combinatorics: arrangements, combinations, the pigeonhole principle, derangements."],"groups":["Group axioms: closure, associativity, identity, inverses.","Order of a group is its number of elements; order of an element $g$ is the smallest $n$ with $g^n=e$.","Cyclic groups have a generator.","Lagrange: the order of a subgroup divides the order of the group.","Isomorphism: a bijection that preserves the operation. Compare element orders to decide."],"complex":["Loci: circles of Apollonius $|z-a|=k|z-b|$ and arcs $\\arg\\dfrac{z-a}{z-b}=\\theta$.","Transformations $w=f(z)$ such as $w=\\dfrac{az+b}{cz+d}$: write $z$ in terms of $w$, then use the locus of $z$ to find the locus of $w$.","Lines and circles map to lines or circles.","Regions: map their boundaries, then test a point."],"recur":["First order: $u_{n+1}=au_n+b$. The solution is the CF $Ca^n$ plus a constant particular solution.","Second order: $u_{n+2}+pu_{n+1}+qu_n=f(n)$. Auxiliary equation $m^2+pm+q=0$. Repeated root: $(A+Bn)m^n$.","Choose a particular solution like $f(n)$; if it clashes with the CF, multiply by $n$.","Prove closed forms by induction. Model problems such as populations and loans."],"matrix":["Eigenvalues: $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0$. Eigenvectors: $(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf v=\\mathbf0$.","Diagonalise $A=PDP^{-1}$ with eigenvectors as the columns of $P$; then $A^n=PD^nP^{-1}$.","Symmetric matrices: orthogonal eigenvectors; normalise them so $P^{-1}=P^T$.","Cayley–Hamilton: $A$ satisfies its own characteristic equation — use it for $A^{-1}$ or $A^n$."],"integ":["Reduction formulae: integrate by parts to express $I_n$ in terms of $I_{n-1}$ or $I_{n-2}$, then apply repeatedly.","Arc length: $s=\\int\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^2}dx$ or $\\int\\sqrt{\\dot x^2+\\dot y^2}\\,dt$.","Surface area of revolution about the $x$-axis: $S=2\\pi\\int y\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^2}dx$.","Polar forms use $\\sqrt{r^2+\\left(\\frac{dr}{d\\theta}\\right)^2}$."]},"stats":{"s1_1":["The population is the whole group; a sample is part of it; a census measures every member.","Random sampling: simple random, systematic (every $k$th), stratified (proportional to group sizes).","Non-random: quota (fill quotas, not random) and opportunity (whoever is available — quick but biased).","Data: qualitative or quantitative; discrete or continuous.","Large data set: know the stations, variables, units and abbreviations such as \"tr\" (trace)."],"s1_2":["Mean $\\bar x=\\dfrac{\\sum x}{n}$ or $\\dfrac{\\sum fx}{\\sum f}$. The median and quartiles use interpolation for grouped data.","Variance $\\sigma^2=\\dfrac{\\sum x^2}{n}-\\bar x^2$; standard deviation $=\\sqrt{\\text{variance}}$. Use $S_{xx}=\\sum x^2-\\frac{(\\sum x)^2}n$.","IQR $=Q_3-Q_1$. The range is affected by outliers.","Coding $y=\\frac{x-a}{b}$: $\\bar x=b\\bar y+a$ and $\\sigma_x=b\\sigma_y$ (adding $a$ doesn't change the spread)."],"s1_3":["Outlier rules, e.g. more than $1.5\\times$ IQR beyond a quartile, or more than $\\bar x\\pm2\\sigma$.","Box plots show the median, quartiles, whiskers and outliers.","Histograms: frequency density $=\\dfrac{\\text{frequency}}{\\text{class width}}$; area is proportional to frequency.","Cumulative frequency: plot at upper class boundaries and read off the median and quartiles.","Comparing data: compare a measure of location AND a measure of spread, in context."],"s1_4":["Correlation: positive, negative or none. It does not imply causation.","Regression line $y=a+bx$: $b$ is the change in $y$ per unit of $x$, and $a$ is the value when $x=0$.","$x$ is the explanatory (independent) variable and $y$ the response (dependent) variable.","Only interpolate; extrapolation is unreliable."],"s1_5":["$P(A')=1-P(A)$. Mutually exclusive: $P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)$.","Independent: $P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)$.","Venn diagrams: fill from the middle outwards; the total is $1$.","Tree diagrams: multiply along branches and add the outcomes you want."],"s1_6":["Discrete distribution: probabilities sum to $1$.","$X\\sim B(n,p)$ needs a fixed $n$, independent trials, two outcomes and a constant $p$.","$P(X=r)=\\binom nr p^r(1-p)^{n-r}$.","Cumulative tables or the calculator: $P(X\\ge r)=1-P(X\\le r-1)$. Take care with \"more than\" and \"at least\"."],"s1_7":["$H_0$ is the null hypothesis (e.g. $p=0.3$); $H_1$ is one-tailed ($p>0.3$) or two-tailed ($p\\neq0.3$).","Critical region: values that reject $H_0$; the actual significance level is the probability of falling in it.","For a two-tailed test, halve the significance level for each tail.","Conclude in context: \"There is (in)sufficient evidence to suggest…\""],"s2_1":["Exponential models: $y=ab^x$ gives $\\log y=\\log a+x\\log b$ (linear in $x$); $y=ax^n$ gives $\\log y=\\log a+n\\log x$.","PMCC $r$ ranges from $-1$ to $1$ and measures linear correlation.","Test for zero correlation: $H_0:\\rho=0$, $H_1:\\rho>0$, $\\rho<0$ or $\\rho\\neq0$; compare $|r|$ with the table's critical value."],"s2_2":["Set notation: $\\cap$ (and), $\\cup$ (or), $A'$ (not).","Conditional probability $P(A\\mid B)=\\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}$.","Independent iff $P(A\\mid B)=P(A)$.","$P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B)$.","On a tree diagram, the second branches are conditional probabilities."],"s2_3":["$X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ is symmetric and bell-shaped; about 68%, 95% and 99.7% lie within $1$, $2$ and $3\\sigma$ of the mean.","Standardise with $Z=\\dfrac{X-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\sim N(0,1)$. Use the inverse normal to find $\\mu$ or $\\sigma$ (simultaneous equations if both are unknown).","Approximating $B(n,p)$ by $N(np,\\,np(1-p))$ needs $n$ large and $p$ near $0.5$. Use a continuity correction (e.g. $P(X\\le 5)\\to P(Y<5.5)$).","Hypothesis test for the mean: $\\bar X\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}n\\right)$."]},"mech":{"m1_8":["Particle: dimensions ignored. Rod: rigid, length only. Lamina: flat. Light: mass ignored. Inextensible: constant length. Smooth: no friction.","Base SI units: kg, m, s. Derived units: N, m s$^{-2}$.","$g=9.8$ m s$^{-2}$ unless told otherwise. Weight $W=mg$.","Know how to refine a model, e.g. include air resistance."],"m1_9":["Displacement–time graph: gradient is velocity. Velocity–time graph: gradient is acceleration, area is displacement.","suvat (constant $a$ only): $v=u+at$, $s=\\tfrac{(u+v)}2t$, $s=ut+\\tfrac12at^2$, $v^2=u^2+2as$, $s=vt-\\tfrac12at^2$.","Vertical motion: $a=-g$ if up is positive. Choose a positive direction and stick to it.","At max height $v=0$; symmetric time up and down only if it lands at the same level."],"m1_10":["Force diagrams: weight, normal reaction, tension/thrust, friction, driving force, resistance.","$F=ma$ using the resultant force. Forces as vectors: $\\mathbf F=m\\mathbf a$.","Connected particles: either treat the whole system (internal tension cancels) or each particle separately.","Pulleys: smooth pulley means equal tension each side; the string is taut and inextensible, so the particles share the same acceleration.","After a string breaks, the particle moves under the remaining forces — e.g. it decelerates under gravity."],"m1_11":["$v=\\dfrac{ds}{dt}$, $a=\\dfrac{dv}{dt}=\\dfrac{d^2s}{dt^2}$.","Integrate the other way: $s=\\int v\\,dt$ and $v=\\int a\\,dt$, using the initial conditions to find $c$.","Max/min displacement or velocity: set the derivative to $0$.","Total distance: split at the times when $v=0$ and add the magnitudes."],"m2_4":["Moment $=$ force $\\times$ perpendicular distance from the pivot.","Equilibrium: the resultant force is $0$ AND the total moment about any point is $0$.","Take moments about a point where an unknown force acts to eliminate it.","Non-uniform rod: the centre of mass isn't at the middle — find it using moments.","On the point of tilting, the reaction at the other support is $0$."],"m2_5":["Resolve forces parallel and perpendicular to the plane: weight components $mg\\sin\\alpha$ (down the slope) and $mg\\cos\\alpha$ (into the slope).","Friction $F\\le\\mu R$; $F=\\mu R$ when moving or in limiting equilibrium.","Friction opposes the (likely) motion.","If a force acts at an angle, the reaction $R$ changes — always resolve to find $R$."],"m2_6":["Horizontal: constant velocity $u\\cos\\alpha$. Vertical: $a=-g$, initial velocity $u\\sin\\alpha$.","Time of flight: when vertical displacement $=0$ (or the given height). Range $=u\\cos\\alpha\\times T$.","Greatest height: vertical $v=0$.","Trajectory: $y=x\\tan\\alpha-\\dfrac{gx^2}{2u^2\\cos^2\\alpha}$.","Speed at a point: $\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}$; direction $\\arctan\\frac{v_y}{v_x}$."],"m2_7":["Static particles: resolve in two directions, or use a triangle of forces.","Limiting friction in equilibrium: $F=\\mu R$, acting opposite to the impending motion.","Ladders: friction at the ground (and maybe the wall). Take moments about the foot.","Connected particles on slopes: write an equation for each particle, with the same acceleration magnitude."],"m2_8":["Vectors in kinematics: $\\mathbf r=\\mathbf r_0+\\mathbf ut+\\tfrac12\\mathbf at^2$, $\\mathbf v=\\mathbf u+\\mathbf at$.","Variable acceleration: $\\mathbf v=\\dfrac{d\\mathbf r}{dt}$, $\\mathbf a=\\dfrac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}$ — differentiate or integrate each component.","Speed $=|\\mathbf v|$. Bearing or direction from the components.","\"Due north\" means the $\\mathbf i$ component is $0$; parallel to $\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j$ means the components are equal."]}};